# acc.add_product(x, y) of Accumulator<T, 2> (milestone2.md, "The accumulator"): acc_mul_2 on the
# limbs of x and y guarded (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation: architecture.md). acc_mul_2
# only multiplies them, in mul_2_2 and in the sum of the magnitudes, which R3 alone does not guard;
# but a compiler that sees one of them to be 1 rewrites fma(x, 1, s) as x + s and fuses a product of
# the program into it (measured in acc_mul_1, the product of one limb, with Apple clang 21 under
# -ffp-contract=fast). After the guards each limb is a number of the format. Its claims are those
# of acc_mul_2: the guard is the identity on numbers of the format.
kernel:        acc_mul_entry_2
input:         s = s0 s1
input:         stored a
input:         supplied x = x0 x1
input:         supplied y = y0 y1
output:        t = t0 t1
output:        b
value:         s+x*y
requires:      normalized s
requires:      normalized x
requires:      normalized y
requires:      nonneg a
ensures:       normalized t
ensures:       error t T c=3*(1+4*u*u)/(1-4*u)+4 k=2 t=2
ensures:       rounded b nearest value=a+abs(x[0])*abs(y[0])
theorem:       proofs/theories/AccKernels.v acc_mul_entry_2_correct
ieee_spec_theorem: proofs/theories/IeeeAccKernels.v acc_mul_entry_2_ieee_correct
min_precision: 4
source:        acc_mul_2 on the guarded limbs of x and y (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation)
license:       algorithm: published; proof: MIT
status:        proved
ieee:          spec
entry:         yes
cone_exempt:   b, the sum of magnitudes, does not reach t0: its claim is read where b is finite (an entry about an output group other than the first), and where b is +inf the mask ok of acc_radius_k is false

gx0          = guard(x0)
gx1          = guard(x1)
gy0          = guard(y0)
gy1          = guard(y1)
(t0, t1, b)  = acc_mul_2(s0, s1, a, gx0, gx1, gy0, gy1)
